latest_plan = 'Based on our revised understanding, here\'s a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Create a deep copy of the input grid to work on.\n\n2. Identify all green (3) and sky blue (8) squares in the grid, storing their coordinates.\n\n3. Calculate a "sky blue contact score" for each possible 2x2 area in the grid:\n   - Score is the number of unique sky blue squares adjacent (including diagonally) to the 2x2 area.\n   - Store these scores in a 2D array for quick lookup.\n\n4. Identify all possible 2x2 green square formations in the current grid configuration.\n\n5. For each 2x2 green formation and individual green square not part of a 2x2:\n   a. Calculate potential new positions across the grid, prioritizing:\n      - Positions with higher sky blue contact scores.\n      - Positions more to the left and up, but not exclusively.\n   b. Rank these potential positions based on their scores and location.\n\n6. Iterate through the ranked potential moves:\n   a. For each move, check if it\'s valid:\n      - The target position is empty or only partially occupied by the same green group.\n      - The move doesn\'t overlap with other green squares.\n      - The move maintains contact with at least one sky blue square.\n   b. If valid, apply the move:\n      - Update the grid, moving the green squares to the new position.\n      - Update the list of green square coordinates.\n      - Recalculate affected sky blue contact scores.\n\n7. After each round of moves, check for any remaining valid moves:\n   - If moves were made, go back to step 4 and repeat the process.\n   - If no moves were made, proceed to step 8.\n\n8. Handle any remaining individual green squares:\n   a. For each individual green square, find the best adjacent position (including diagonal) that:\n      - Maximizes contact with sky blue squares.\n      - Moves towards the left and up if possible.\n   b. Move the square if a better position is found.\n\n9. Repeat steps 4-8 until no more moves are possible.\n\n10. As a final optimization step, check if any 2x2 green formations can be split to increase overall sky blue contact:\n    - For each 2x2 formation, calculate if splitting it would result in higher total sky blue contact.\n    - If so, split the formation and move the squares to their optimal positions.\n\n11. Return the transformed grid.\n\nAdditional considerations:\n\n- Implement a function to quickly check sky blue adjacency for any given position.\n- Use a priority queue to efficiently manage and update the ranked potential moves.\n- Implement a function to check if a move maintains the integrity of green square groups (for moves involving multiple squares).\n- Keep track of areas of the grid that have been optimized to avoid unnecessary rechecks.\n\nThis plan takes into account the need to form 2x2 green squares where possible, distribute them across the grid to maximize sky blue contact, and allow for both grouping and splitting of green squares. It also considers the preference for left and up movements without being strictly bound by them. The iterative nature of the process ensures that the grid reaches a stable, optimized state.'
